      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m worried this builds the wrong table for a 4KB process once the buffer crosses a 16KB page. `vb2_create_framevec()` now returns one entry per 4KB page (native pages repeat, each with its own slice in the span), but `sg_alloc_table_from_pages()` only uses the first offset and treats every later entry as a whole 16KB page from offset 0.\n\nWith pages `[A, A, A, A, B, ...]`, for example, the second entry maps all of `A` again instead of `B`. Since `n_pages` counts 4KB pages, `size` also underflows partway and the remaining entries get full 16KB lengths, so DMA can land in slices outside the buffer.\n\n`vm_map_ram()` in `vb2_dma_sg_vaddr()`, and in `vb2_vmalloc_get_userptr()` which this patch doesn\u0027t touch, maps the repeated pages the same way. The `vb2_userptr_ppps` test in aosp/4367986 only uses two slices of one native page, so it wouldn\u0027t catch this. Native processes look unchanged.\n\nWith 16KB kernel pages I don\u0027t think `vm_map_ram()` can map scattered 4KB slices contiguously at all, so rejecting USERPTR for 4KB processes, as aosp/4368000 does, looks like the safer fix to me.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "32a9d8df0ce300716342dcf1e51f70ed1612fe92",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
